# 5. AWS Infrastructure

The Terraform infrastructure consists of:

* VPC
* Public Subnet
* Internet Gateway
* Route Table
* Route Table Association
* Security Group
* EC2 Instance
* Elastic IP
* S3 Bucket
* S3 Bucket Versioning

---

# 6. VPC Configuration

A dedicated VPC was created for the ContentSphere project.

CIDR:

```text
10.0.0.0/16
```

The VPC provides the network environment for the ContentSphere infrastructure.

DNS support and DNS hostnames were enabled.

---

# 7. Public Subnet

A public subnet was created inside the VPC.

CIDR:

```text
10.0.1.0/24
```

Availability Zone:

```text
ap-south-1a
```

The subnet was configured to assign public IP addresses to resources launched within it.

---

# 8. Internet Gateway and Route Table

An Internet Gateway was attached to the VPC.

A route table was created with:

```text
Destination: 0.0.0.0/0
Target: Internet Gateway
```

This allows resources in the public subnet to communicate with the internet.

---

# 9. Security Group

A security group was created for the EC2 instance.

The following inbound ports were configured:

| Port | Protocol | Purpose |
| ---- | -------- | ------- |
| 22   | TCP      | SSH     |
| 80   | TCP      | HTTP    |

Outbound traffic was allowed so that the EC2 instance could communicate with required external services.

---

# 10. EC2 Instance

An EC2 instance was provisioned using Terraform.

Configuration:

```text
Instance Type: t3.micro
Region: ap-south-1
```

The EC2 instance was configured using Terraform user data.

Apache HTTP Server was installed automatically during instance initialization.

---

# 11. ContentSphere Website

Terraform user data automatically installs Apache and creates the ContentSphere website.

The website contains:

```text
ContentSphere

Infrastructure Provisioned using Terraform

Student: Essakimuthu Shanmugam Konar
Batch: IT Vedant
Region: ap-south-1
Environment: dev
```

This demonstrates that Terraform can provision both infrastructure and initial server configuration.

---

# 12. Elastic IP

An Elastic IP was associated with the EC2 instance.

The Elastic IP provides a stable public address for accessing the ContentSphere web server.

---

# 13. S3 Storage

An S3 bucket was created for ContentSphere.

The project bucket is used as AWS object storage and demonstrates Terraform management of S3 resources.

S3 bucket versioning was enabled.

---

# 14. Terraform Modules

Reusable Terraform modules were created to improve project organization and reusability.

The project contains:

```text
modules/
├── vpc/
├── security-group/
├── ec2/
└── s3/
```

Each module contains:

```text
main.tf
variables.tf
outputs.tf
```

The root `main.tf` calls these modules.

This structure allows the same modules to be reused in future DevOps projects.

---

# 15. Variables

Terraform variables were used to avoid hardcoding infrastructure configuration.

Examples include:

```text
project_name
environment
vpc_cidr
subnet_cidr
availability_zone
ami_id
instance_type
key_name
```

The actual values are supplied through:

```text
terraform.tfvars
```

---

# 16. Terraform Outputs

Terraform outputs were created to display important infrastructure information.

Examples:

```text
VPC ID
Subnet ID
Security Group ID
EC2 Instance ID
Public IP
Elastic IP
S3 Bucket Name
```

This makes it easier to identify and use provisioned AWS resources.

---

# 17. Terraform Lifecycle

The Terraform lifecycle was demonstrated using:

```bash
terraform init
terraform validate
terraform plan
terraform apply
terraform output
terraform state list
```

Terraform was used to create and manage the infrastructure.

Terraform can also remove infrastructure using:

```bash
terraform destroy
```

---

# 18. Terraform State Management

Terraform maintains a state file that records the infrastructure managed by Terraform.

The state allows Terraform to compare the desired configuration with the actual infrastructure in AWS.

The state resources were organized using module addresses.

Example:

```text
module.vpc.aws_vpc.this
module.vpc.aws_subnet.this
module.security_group.aws_security_group.this
module.ec2.aws_instance.this
module.ec2.aws_eip.this
module.s3.aws_s3_bucket.this
```

---

# 19. Remote State

Remote Terraform state was configured using Amazon S3.

Remote state bucket:

```text
contentsphere-terraform-state-2026-essaki
```

State path:

```text
contentsphere/terraform.tfstate
```

S3 versioning was enabled.

Remote state provides centralized state storage and helps prevent loss of the local state file.

---

# 20. Infrastructure Drift

Infrastructure drift occurs when the actual AWS infrastructure differs from the configuration managed by Terraform.

A test tag was manually added to the EC2 instance outside Terraform:

```text
DriftTest = ChangedOutsideTerraform
```

Terraform was then executed using:

```bash
terraform plan
```

Terraform detected the external change and showed that the manually added tag was not present in the Terraform configuration.

This successfully demonstrated infrastructure drift detection.

---

# 21. AWS CloudFormation

An equivalent ContentSphere infrastructure template was created using AWS CloudFormation.

File:

```text
cloudformation/contentsphere-stack.yaml
```

The template defines:

* VPC
* Subnet
* Internet Gateway
* Route Table
* Security Group
* EC2
* S3

The template was first validated using AWS CLI.

Command:

```bash
aws cloudformation validate-template --template-body file://cloudformation/contentsphere-stack.yaml
```

---

# 22. CloudFormation Deployment

The CloudFormation template was deployed from the VS Code PowerShell terminal using AWS CLI.

Deployment command:

```bash
aws cloudformation deploy --template-file cloudformation/contentsphere-stack.yaml --stack-name ContentSphere-CF-Demo --parameter-overrides KeyName=terraform Environment=dev --region ap-south-1
```

The resulting CloudFormation stack was:

```text
ContentSphere-CF-Demo
```

The stack successfully reached:

```text
CREATE_COMPLETE
```

This confirms that ContentSphere infrastructure was successfully demonstrated using both Terraform and AWS CloudFormation.

---

# 23. Terraform and CloudFormation Comparison

| Feature         | Terraform         | CloudFormation            |
| --------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------- |
| Developer       | HashiCorp         | AWS                       |
| Configuration   | HCL               | YAML/JSON                 |
| Cloud Support   | Multi-cloud       | AWS                       |
| State           | Terraform State   | Managed by CloudFormation |
| Modules         | Terraform Modules | Nested Stacks             |
| AWS Integration | Very Good         | Native                    |
| Main Purpose    | General IaC       | AWS-native IaC            |

Terraform was selected as the primary tool for the project because of its reusable module system and multi-cloud capability.

CloudFormation was implemented to demonstrate AWS-native Infrastructure as Code.

---

# 24. AWS CDK

AWS CDK is another AWS Infrastructure as Code tool that allows infrastructure to be defined using programming languages such as TypeScript, Python, Java, C#, and Go.

For this project, CDK was reviewed conceptually and compared with Terraform and CloudFormation.

A separate CDK deployment was not implemented because Terraform was the primary IaC implementation and CloudFormation was used as the AWS-native comparison.

---

# 25. Git and GitHub

The project was organized as a Git repository.

The repository contains:

```text
README.md
Terraform files
Terraform modules
CloudFormation template
Screenshots
Project documentation
```

Sensitive files such as:

```text
terraform.tfvars
*.tfstate
.terraform/
```

are excluded using `.gitignore`.

